WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.770
<v Instructor>So disaster recovery</v>

2
00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:04.740
as a solutions architect is super important,

3
00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.070
and the exam expects you to know about disaster recovery.

4
00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.110
And there's a white paper on it, you should read it,

5
00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.690
but I try to summarize everything clearly

6
00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.850
with graphs and diagrams in this lecture,

7
00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:17.100
so you don't have to read it if you don't want to.

8
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.050
But overall, you can expect some question

9
00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.480
on disaster recovery, and as a solutions architect,

10
00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.940
you need to know about disaster recovery anyway.

11
00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.380
Don't worry, I try to make this

12
00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:27.360
as simple as possible for you.

13
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.860
So what is a disaster?

14
00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:31.230
Well, it's any event that has a negative impact

15
00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:34.260
on the company's business continuity or finances.

16
00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.595
And so disaster recovery is about preparing

17
00:00:36.595 --> 00:00:39.090
and recovering from these disasters.

18
00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:42.206
So what kind of disaster recovery can we do on AWS

19
00:00:42.206 --> 00:00:43.800
or in general?

20
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:46.290
Well, we can do on-premise to on-premise.

21
00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:48.900
That means we have a first data center, maybe in California,

22
00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.000
another data center maybe in Seattle.

23
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.737
And so this is traditional disaster recovery,

24
00:00:52.737 --> 00:00:55.230
and it's actually very, very expensive.

25
00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:56.730
Or we can start using the cloud

26
00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:59.220
and do on-premise as a main data center.

27
00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:02.255
And then if we have any disaster, use the cloud.

28
00:01:02.255 --> 00:01:03.690
So this is called hybrid recovery,

29
00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:05.910
or if you're just all in the cloud,

30
00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:08.850
then you can do AWS cloud region A to cloud region B,

31
00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:12.060
and that would be a full cloud type of disaster recovery.

32
00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:13.530
Now, before we do disaster recovery,

33
00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:14.880
we need to define two key terms,

34
00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.520
and you need to understand them from an exam perspective.

35
00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.940
The first one is called RPO, Recovery Point Objective,

36
00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:25.200
and the second one is called RTO, Recovery Time Objective.

37
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.340
So remember these two terms,

38
00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.170
and I'm going to explain them right now.

39
00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:30.420
So what is RPO and RTO?

40
00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.510
The first one is the RPO, Recovery Point Objective.

41
00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:34.920
And so this is basically

42
00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:37.320
how often basically you run backups.

43
00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.966
How back in time can you to recover?

44
00:01:39.966 --> 00:01:42.960
And when a disaster strikes, basically,

45
00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:45.150
the time between their RPO

46
00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:47.580
and the disaster is going to be a data loss.

47
00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:51.060
For example, if you back up data every hour

48
00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.850
and a disaster strikes, then you can go back in time

49
00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:57.300
for an hour and so you'll have lost one hour of data.

50
00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.610
So the RPO, sometimes it can be an hour,

51
00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:01.200
sometimes maybe one minute.

52
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:02.670
It really depends on the requirements.

53
00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:05.280
But RPO is how much of a data loss

54
00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:08.864
are you willing to accept in case a disaster happens.

55
00:02:08.864 --> 00:02:10.800
RTO, on the other end,

56
00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.830
is when you recover from your disaster, okay?

57
00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:16.770
And so between the disaster and the RTO

58
00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:19.560
is the amount of downtime your application has.

59
00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.020
So sometimes it's okay to have 24 hours of downtime,

60
00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:23.010
I don't think it is.

61
00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:24.420
Sometimes it's not okay,

62
00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:27.660
and maybe you need just one minute of downtime, okay?

63
00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:30.660
So basically optimizing for the RPO and the RTO

64
00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:33.330
does drive some solution architecture decisions,

65
00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:35.940
and obviously, the smaller you want these things to be,

66
00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:37.950
usually the higher the cost.

67
00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.680
So let's talk about disaster recovery strategies.

68
00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:42.690
The first one is Backup and Restore.

69
00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:45.900
Second one is Pilot Light. Third one is Warm Standby.

70
00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:49.290
And fourth one is Hot Site or Multi Site Approach.

71
00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.720
So if we basically rank them,

72
00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:53.790
all will have different RTO.

73
00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:56.340
Backup and Restore will have the smaller RTO.

74
00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.740
Pilot Light then Warm Standby, and Multi Site,

75
00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:02.040
all these things cost more money, but they get a faster RTO.

76
00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.470
That means you have less downtime overall.

77
00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:06.930
So let's look at all of these one by one

78
00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:08.190
in details to really understand

79
00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:10.890
from an architectural standpoint what they mean.

80
00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.770
Backup and Restore has a high RPO.

81
00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:15.330
That means that you have a corporate data center,

82
00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:18.210
for example, and here is your a AWS's Cloud,

83
00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:19.500
and you have an S3 bucket.

84
00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.900
And so if you want to back up your data over time,

85
00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.139
maybe we can use AWS's Storage Gateway

86
00:03:24.139 --> 00:03:26.970
or and have some lifecycle policy,

87
00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:30.150
put data into Glacier for cost optimization purposes.

88
00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:33.300
Or maybe once a week, you're sending a ton of data

89
00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.780
into Glacier using AWS's Snowball.

90
00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:38.460
So here, you know, if you use Snowball,

91
00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:40.560
your RPO is gonna be about one week,

92
00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.750
because if your data center burns or whatever

93
00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:44.400
and you lose all your data, then you've lost one week

94
00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.496
of data because you send that Snowball device once a week.

95
00:03:47.496 --> 00:03:49.950
If you're using the AWS's cloud instead,

96
00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:52.350
maybe EBS volumes, Redshift, and RDS.

97
00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:56.100
If you schedule regular snapshots and you back them up,

98
00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:59.220
then your RPO is going to be maybe 24 hours

99
00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:01.260
or one hour based on how frequently

100
00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:04.290
you do create these snapshots.

101
00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:07.170
And then when you have a disaster strike you

102
00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:09.960
and you need to basically restore all your data,

103
00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:13.380
then you can use AMIs to recreate EC2 instances

104
00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:14.790
and speed up your applications.

105
00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:16.620
Or you can restore straight from a snapshot

106
00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:19.830
and recreate your Amazon RDS database or your EBS volume,

107
00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:21.919
or your Redshift, whatever you want.

108
00:04:21.919 --> 00:04:23.679
And so that can take a lot of time as well to restore

109
00:04:23.679 --> 00:04:27.810
this data and so you get a high RTO as well.

110
00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.490
But the reason we do this is actually

111
00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:31.650
it's quite cheap to do Backup and Restore.

112
00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:33.360
We don't manage infrastructure in the middle.

113
00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:35.250
We just recreate infrastructure

114
00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:37.080
when we need it, when we have a disaster.

115
00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.390
And so the only cost we have

116
00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:41.910
is the cost of storing these backups.

117
00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:43.920
So it gives you an idea. Backup and Restore.

118
00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.740
Very easy, not too expensive,

119
00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:49.061
and you get high RPO, high RTO.

120
00:04:49.061 --> 00:04:52.230
The second one is going to be Pilot Light.

121
00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:53.850
So here with Pilot Light,

122
00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:57.570
a small version of the app is always running in the cloud.

123
00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:00.090
And so usually that's going to be your critical core.

124
00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:01.980
And this is what it's called Pilot Lights.

125
00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:03.900
So it's very similar to Backup and Restore,

126
00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:06.660
but this time, it's faster because your critical systems,

127
00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:07.950
they're already up and running.

128
00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:11.040
And so when you do recover, you just need to add on

129
00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.890
all the other systems that are not as critical.

130
00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:15.060
So let's have an example.

131
00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:17.910
This is your data center has a server and a database

132
00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:19.350
and this is the AWS cloud.

133
00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:22.380
Maybe you're going to do continuous data replication

134
00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:24.780
from your critical database into RDS,

135
00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:26.310
which is going to be running at any time.

136
00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:29.760
So you get an RDS database ready to go running,

137
00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.540
but your EC2 instances, they're not critical just yet.

138
00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:34.980
What's really important is your data.

139
00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:36.330
And so they're not running,

140
00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:38.670
but in case you have a disaster happening,

141
00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:41.460
Route 53 will allow you to fail over from your server

142
00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:44.730
on your data center, recreate that EC2 instance in the cloud

143
00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:45.840
and make it up and running.

144
00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:48.780
But your RDS database is already ready.

145
00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:50.100
So here, what do we get?

146
00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:53.790
Well, we get a lower RPO, we get a lower RTO,

147
00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:54.930
and we still manage costs.

148
00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:56.730
We still have to have an RDS running,

149
00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:59.670
but just the RDS database is running, the rest is not.

150
00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:03.960
And your EC2 instance only are created

151
00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:06.090
when you need to do a disaster recovery.

152
00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:08.370
So Pilot Light is a very popular choice.

153
00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:11.520
Remember, it's only for critical core systems.

154
00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:15.240
Warm Standby is when you have a full system up and running,

155
00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:16.380
but at a minimum size.

156
00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:18.870
So it's ready to go, but upon disaster,

157
00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:21.240
we can scale it to production load.

158
00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:22.320
So let's have a look.

159
00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:23.550
We have our corporate data center.

160
00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:25.020
Maybe it's a bit more complicated this time.

161
00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:26.765
We have a reverse proxy, an app server,

162
00:06:26.765 --> 00:06:28.710
and a primary database.

163
00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:31.530
And currently, our Route 53 is pointing the DNS

164
00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:32.940
to our corporate data center.

165
00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:35.910
And in the cloud, we'll still have our data replication

166
00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:38.430
to an RDS secondary database that is running.

167
00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:40.950
And maybe we'll have an EC2 Auto Scaling group,

168
00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:42.840
but running at minimum capacity.

169
00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:44.160
That's currently talking

170
00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.740
to our corporate data center database.

171
00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:49.740
And maybe we have an ELB as well, ready to go.

172
00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:51.780
And so if a disaster strikes you,

173
00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:53.490
because we have a Warm Standby,

174
00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:56.967
we can use Route 53 to failover to the ELB,

175
00:06:56.967 --> 00:07:00.330
and we can use the failover to also change

176
00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:02.790
where our application is getting our data from.

177
00:07:02.790 --> 00:07:05.520
Maybe it's getting the data now from the other RDS,

178
00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:07.860
and so we've effectively basically stood by

179
00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:09.150
and then maybe using auto scaling,

180
00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:11.460
our application will scale pretty quickly.

181
00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:14.100
So this is a more costly thing to do now

182
00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:16.701
because we already have an ELB

183
00:07:16.701 --> 00:07:17.940
and EC2 Scaling running at any time.

184
00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:19.920
But again, you can decrease your RPO

185
00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:21.540
and your RTO doing that.

186
00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:25.800
And then finally, we get the Multi Site/Hot Site approach.

187
00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:27.030
It's very low RTO.

188
00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:28.530
We're talking minutes or seconds,

189
00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:30.120
but it's also very expensive.

190
00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:32.400
But you get two full production scales

191
00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:33.750
running on AWS and on-premise.

192
00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:36.150
So that means that we have your on-premise data center,

193
00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:37.290
full production scale.

194
00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:40.260
You have your AWS data center, full production scale

195
00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:42.060
with some data replication happening.

196
00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:43.470
And so here, what happens is that

197
00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:45.630
because you have a hot site that's already running,

198
00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:47.250
your Route 53 can route request

199
00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:50.070
to both your corporate data center and the AWS cloud.

200
00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:52.200
It's called an active-active type of setup.

201
00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:55.530
And so the idea here is that the failover can happen.

202
00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:57.030
You know, your EC2 can failover

203
00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:59.010
to your RDS database if need be,

204
00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:00.840
but you get full production scale running

205
00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:02.877
on AWS and on premise.

206
00:08:02.877 --> 00:08:04.830
And so this costs a lot of money,

207
00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:07.380
but at the same time, you're ready to failover already

208
00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:10.470
and you're running into a multi-DC type of infrastructure,

209
00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:11.940
which is quite cool.

210
00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:14.430
Finally, if you wanted to go all cloud,

211
00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:16.410
you know, it would be the same kind of architecture.

212
00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:17.430
It would be a multi region.

213
00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:19.230
So maybe we could use Aurora here

214
00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:20.940
because we're really in the cloud.

215
00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:23.640
So we have a primary database in a region,

216
00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:25.620
and then we have a Aurora global database

217
00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:27.570
that's being replicated to another region.

218
00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:29.520
These both regions are working for me,

219
00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:31.230
and then when I want to failover, you know,

220
00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:33.540
I will be ready to go full production scale again

221
00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:35.310
in another region if I need to.

222
00:08:35.310 --> 00:08:36.450
So this gives you an idea

223
00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:39.000
of all the strategies you can have for disaster recovery.

224
00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:41.040
It's really up to you to select

225
00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:42.840
the disaster recovery strategy you need.

226
00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:44.580
But the exam will ask you basically

227
00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:47.370
based on some scenarios, you know, what do you recommend?

228
00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:49.830
Do you recommend Backup and Restore, Pilot Lights?

229
00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:52.590
Do you recommend Multi Site or do you recommend Hot Site?

230
00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:54.240
All that kind of stuff.

231
00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:55.710
Warm Backups and all that stuff.

232
00:08:55.710 --> 00:08:58.470
Okay, so finally, disaster recovery tips.

233
00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:00.450
And it's more like, real life stuff.

234
00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:02.790
So for backups, you can use EBS Snapshots,

235
00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:05.220
RDS automated Snapshots and backups, et cetera.

236
00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:07.260
And you can push all these Snapshots regularly

237
00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:09.540
to S3, S3 IA Glacier.

238
00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:11.130
You can implement a Lifecycle Policy,

239
00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:13.350
you can use Cross Region Replication if you wanted

240
00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:15.690
to make sure these backups would be in different regions.

241
00:09:15.690 --> 00:09:17.820
And if you wanted to have your data from on-premise

242
00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:19.770
to the cloud, Snowball or Storage Gateway

243
00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:21.270
would be great technologies.

244
00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:23.880
For high availability, using Route 53

245
00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:25.860
to migrate DNS from a region to another region

246
00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:28.620
is really, really helpful and easy to implement.

247
00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:30.000
But you can also use technology

248
00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.697
to have multi easy implemented, such as RDS Multi-AZ,

249
00:09:32.697 --> 00:09:36.180
ElastiCache Multi AZ, EFS, S3,

250
00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:39.030
all these things are highly available by default

251
00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:40.442
if you enable them.

252
00:09:40.442 --> 00:09:43.392
Obviously, if you are talking about the higher availability

253
00:09:44.530 --> 00:09:46.350
of your network, maybe you've implemented Direct Connect

254
00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:48.870
to connect from your corporate data center to AWS,

255
00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:51.390
but what if the connection goes down for whatever reason?

256
00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:53.400
Maybe you can use site-to-site VPN

257
00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:56.070
as a recovery option for your network.

258
00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:57.270
In terms of replication,

259
00:09:57.270 --> 00:09:59.730
you can use RDS Replication (Cross Region),

260
00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:01.530
Aurora + Global Databases.

261
00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:04.590
Maybe you can use a database replication software

262
00:10:04.590 --> 00:10:07.350
to do your on-premise database to RDS.

263
00:10:07.350 --> 00:10:09.540
Or maybe you can use Storage Gateway as well.

264
00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:10.470
In terms of automation,

265
00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:12.870
so how do we, you know, recover from disasters?

266
00:10:12.870 --> 00:10:15.595
I think you would know already CloudFormation,

267
00:10:15.595 --> 00:10:17.550
Elastic Beanstalk can help recreate whole new environments

268
00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:19.320
in the cloud very quickly.

269
00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:21.240
Or maybe if we use CloudWatch,

270
00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:23.850
we can recover or reboot our EC2 instances

271
00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:25.692
when the CloudWatch alarms fail.

272
00:10:25.692 --> 00:10:27.840
AWS Lambda can also be great

273
00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:29.580
to customize optimum automation.

274
00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:31.770
So they're great to do, you know, rest API,

275
00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:33.210
but they can also be used to automate

276
00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:34.950
your entire AWS infrastructure.

277
00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:36.992
And so overall, if you manage

278
00:10:36.992 --> 00:10:39.062
to automate your whole disaster recovery,

279
00:10:39.062 --> 00:10:41.580
then you are really, really well set for success.

280
00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:43.230
And then finally, chaos testing.

281
00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:45.690
So how do we know how to recover from the disaster?

282
00:10:45.690 --> 00:10:47.160
Then you create disasters.

283
00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:50.190
And so an example that's I think widely quoted now

284
00:10:50.190 --> 00:10:52.260
in the AWS world is that Netflix,

285
00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:53.989
they run everything on the AWS

286
00:10:53.989 --> 00:10:57.420
and they have created something called a simian army,

287
00:10:57.420 --> 00:11:00.210
and they randomly terminate EC2 instances, for example.

288
00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:01.440
They do so much more,

289
00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:03.960
but basically, they just take an application server

290
00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:05.070
and terminate it randomly.

291
00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:07.980
In production, okay? Not endeavor test, in production.

292
00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:11.190
So they wanna make sure that their infrastructure is capable

293
00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:12.300
to survive failures,

294
00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:15.420
and so that's why they're running a bunch of chaos monkeys

295
00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:17.310
that just terminate stuff randomly

296
00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:20.310
just to make sure that their infrastructure is rock solid

297
00:11:20.310 --> 00:11:22.830
and can survive any types of failures.

298
00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:25.740
So that's it for this section on disaster recovery.

299
00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:26.573
I hope you enjoyed it

300
00:11:26.573 --> 00:11:28.470
and I will see you in the next lecture.

